<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Universal House of Justice</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">founded <a href="/years/1963">1963</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Universal House of Justice is the international governing body of the Baháʼí Faith, seated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a>, Israel, on the slope of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-carmel">Mount Carmel</a>. Envisioned by Baháʼu'lláh in the Kitáb-i-Aqdas as a body to legislate on matters his writings did not address, it was first elected in 1963 and is elected every five years by the members of the National Spiritual Assemblies, each voting for nine adult men. It guides the worldwide community through multi-year plans and annual Ridván messages and has drawn attention to the persecution of Baháʼís in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iran">Iran</a>. Baháʼís regard its decisions as infallible, and its membership is confined to men.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/universal-house-of-justice.jpg" alt="Universal House of Justice" width="330" height="247" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sarah Haslip · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seat_of_the_House_of_Justice.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…faith places great weight on the Covenant: Bahá’u’lláh named his son ʻAbdu’l-Bahá as the interpreter of his writings, and the faith’s authority passed in turn to his great-grandson Shoghi Effendi and then to the <b>Universal House of Justice</b>, a succession that has preserved the unity of the community.</blockquote><a href="/religion/bahai-faith">Bahá'í Faith · Beliefs &amp; Practice</a></li></ul></section>
